Quick Start on Windows

1. Open the project folder

Open PowerShell in the folder that contains setup_windows.bat:

Plain Text


cd "C:\path\to\QuantGrad_V5_Complete"



2. Complete first-time setup

Run this once to create a compatible Python 3.12 environment and install the backend dependencies:

Plain Text


.\setup_windows.bat



3. Start the model backend

Open a new PowerShell terminal in the project root and run:

Plain Text


.\run_backend.bat



Leave this terminal running. The backend starts at:

Plain Text


http://127.0.0.1:8000



To verify model availability, open another terminal and run:

Plain Text


Invoke-RestMethod http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/status



When saved artifacts are available, the result should show status : ready and artifacts_ready : True.

4. Start the frontend

Open a second new PowerShell terminal in the same project root and run:

Plain Text


.\run_frontend.bat



Then open:

Plain Text


http://localhost:3000




If Windows blocks Corepack or a global pnpm installation, use the provided launcher. It uses npx pnpm@10.4.1, so a global pnpm installation is not required.

Using Saved Models

If backend/artifacts/ already contains the trained artifacts, do not retrain before launching the app. Start the backend and frontend using the commands above.

The inference service requires these saved items:

Plain Text


trend_model.keras
structure_model.keras
entry_model.keras
scaler.pkl
pca.pkl
quantum_params.pkl
artifact_manifest.json



The manifest records SHA-256 hashes for the artifact set. Before enabling the Keras Lambda deserialization required by the saved models, the backend verifies the manifest to help ensure that only explicitly trusted local artifacts are loaded.

Quick Training

If no model artifacts are present, or you deliberately want to create a new quick demonstration model, run:

Plain Text


.\train_quick.bat



The training pipeline automatically creates backend/artifacts/, saves the trained model files, and writes an updated integrity manifest. Training quality depends on the amount and representativeness of the available historical data; a quick run is intended for functional demonstration, not production-grade evaluation.

